WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:29.940
Two kids, ages 4 and 6, made their first $100 and asked ai to help run the stand. This is Models &amp; Agents for Beginners, Episode 137. Picture a wooden stall painted by small hands, a jar filling up with coins, and a helium balloon that suddenly taught a money lesson most adults still skip. Skyloo &amp; Poppy, ages 4 and 6, ran a lemonade stand. They sold out the drinks, moved a stack of hand-painted flower cards, and made

00:00:29.940 --> 00:00:59.920
their first $100. Their dad wrote that the money was nice, but the real prize was inventing the stand, gathering ideas, and figuring out how to actually run it. They learned customer Suras, where money comes from, and that you can build something of your own, instead of only taking a job. They also learned the hard way, because one stretch meant three hours of work and only four sales. A helium balloon that cost six euros suddenly looked different when they realized it equaled about four cups of lemon

00:00:59.920 --> 00:01:29.760
lemonade. That balloon became a translator for what money is actually worth, and a lot of grown-ups still have not learned that lesson. Here is the twist that made me sit up. The family used a free ai tool called Proplexity to help run the stand. ai just means software that can understand regular language and help you write, plan, or build things. Proplexity is an ai you talk to in plain English, a bit like a search engine that answers in full sentences instead of handing you a pile of blue links.

00:01:29.920 --> 00:01:45.660
They did something people call vibe coding, vibe coding just means you describe what you want in regular words, and the ai helps build it. Think of it like telling a friend exactly how you want your Minecraft shop to work, and they snap the blocks into place while you talk.

00:01:45.660 --> 00:02:03.600
Their tiny shop dashboard watched sales and costs, the way a scoreboard watches points in a game. It tracked inventory, which is just how many cups and cards they still had left to sell. It kept customer notes, and it let them add or remove products when something sold out or a new idea showed up.

00:02:03.600 --> 00:02:20.900
Grandpa gave leftover wood, they painted the stand, and some supplies will work again next year, like the cups, the jar, and the decorations. Why should this matter to you? Because this is what ai looks like when it leaves the homework tab and shows up at a real stall with real customers.

00:02:21.500 --> 00:02:29.180
It is not replacing the kid's ideas, it is the clipboard, the calculator, and the notebook that asks whether they actually made a profit.

00:02:29.180 --> 00:02:42.820
Their brains stay on the fun parts, like the plan, the signs, and the talk into customers. If you have ever wanted a bake sale, a sticker shop, a tutoring side hustle, or a club fundraiser, that is the same shape of problem.

00:02:43.480 --> 00:02:53.200
You can try the same kind of helper right now for free with no coding. Open a browser, go to the perplexity website, and you may need a parents help to sign up.

00:02:53.200 --> 00:03:05.000
Type this, I want to run a one day bake sale at school, give me a supply list, prices that could make a profit, and a simple table I can use to track what I sell, what it cost, and what is left.

00:03:05.760 --> 00:03:15.260
Then ask it to make the table even simpler, as if a 10 year old has to fill it in. You will see the same kind of helper those kids used, and you do not need a fancy setup.

00:03:15.260 --> 00:03:23.000
There is a quieter habit hiding inside every creative chat you have with an AI, and it has a simple name, variance.

00:03:23.740 --> 00:03:33.900
Variance is just how mixed and surprising the results are. Give a whole class the same story prompt, and you get vampires, space hamsters, and a sandwich that solves crimes.

00:03:34.620 --> 00:03:40.900
That wild spread of totally different answers is high variance, and it is what makes brainstorming feel alive.

00:03:40.900 --> 00:03:52.720
Now ask an AI the same prompt five times. A lot of the time you get five stories that feel like cousins. Same mood, same plot beats, same dark past, and hard of gold energy.

00:03:53.560 --> 00:03:58.120
Walk with me through a gym dart board, because that is the simplest picture of what is going on.

00:03:58.820 --> 00:04:01.840
First, you throw a prompt at the model the way you throw a dart.

00:04:02.440 --> 00:04:08.760
Second, the model has practiced on so many answers that people called good, so it aims for the middle of the target.

00:04:08.760 --> 00:04:17.880
The answer that usually scores well. Third, five throws land in a tight little cluster, which is why the stories feel related instead of surprising.

00:04:18.620 --> 00:04:24.960
Fourth, the weird arrow that would land in the next county never leaves your hand, unless you specifically ask for it.

00:04:25.500 --> 00:04:31.920
The software is not copying one story off a cheat sheet. It is steering toward the answer that usually looks successful.

00:04:31.920 --> 00:04:35.800
The way a cautious student writes the essay they think the teacher wants.

00:04:36.380 --> 00:04:44.780
Ethan Malik, who writes about how people actually use these tools, argued that AI labs need to care more about that spread on creative work.

00:04:45.260 --> 00:04:53.840
If it takes a lot of extra poking to get real variety out of a smart model, then the model is less useful for brainstorming, problem solving, and art.

00:04:54.460 --> 00:04:57.880
Those are the jobs where the fifth idea is supposed to surprise you.

00:04:57.880 --> 00:05:06.980
So when your chatbot keeps handing you three different slogans that all sound like the same slogan in a new hoodie, that is low variance, not you being picky.

00:05:07.700 --> 00:05:15.700
You can push it on purpose. Ask it this. Give me five options that would disagree with each other. Make one of them slightly unhinged.

00:05:16.020 --> 00:05:26.580
You are not breaking it. You are asking it to step off the bullseye. That is why a brainstorming session can feel stuck, and that is also why a slightly unhinged request can shake it loose.

00:05:26.580 --> 00:05:29.660
You now know the habit, and you can work with it.

00:05:30.240 --> 00:05:36.200
Meanwhile, the unofficial family tech support job just got a serious rival, and it lives on a phone.

00:05:36.940 --> 00:05:43.040
Ethan Malik's father got a new MacBook after years with no laptop, and he did not know how to set it up.

00:05:43.460 --> 00:05:51.020
He opened chatGPT on his phone. ChatGPT is the chatbot a lot of you have already typed into for homework or jokes.

00:05:51.020 --> 00:05:58.960
He asked how to install Codex, which is an AI helper that can actually do tasks on the computer, not just talk about them.

00:05:59.600 --> 00:06:04.200
Then he told Codex to take over, fix setup problems, and install what he needed.

00:06:04.840 --> 00:06:10.760
Malik had been the family's unofficial tech support for decades, and he basically watched that job disappear.

00:06:10.760 --> 00:06:18.460
If a parent or grandparent in your life still texts you that the Wi-Fi looks angry, this is the future of that tech thread.

00:06:19.040 --> 00:06:21.360
You do not need Codex for a first taste.

00:06:22.040 --> 00:06:25.680
On your phone, open chatGPT and try this exact ask.

00:06:26.240 --> 00:06:38.500
Type, I have never done this before. Walk me through changing my laptop's screen brightness and setting up a simple folder for school files, one tap at a time, and wait for me to say done before the next step.

00:06:38.500 --> 00:06:43.280
Watch how it waits for you, like a patient older sibling who will not skip ahead.

00:06:43.800 --> 00:06:47.240
And if setup help is one flavor, homework help is another.

00:06:47.860 --> 00:06:56.740
One student who had only used the three websites for chatGPT and Claude started treating an AI helper as a teacher, not a vending machine for answers.

00:06:57.480 --> 00:07:09.040
Claude is another chatbot, similar idea, different company. The helper planned a whole project, chopped it into 40 exercises, checked the work, and explained sloppy habits versus solid ones.

00:07:09.620 --> 00:07:17.340
It did not just hand over something to copy. They said it felt like learning a new idea in a single day with a patient coach who never gets tired.

00:07:17.980 --> 00:07:22.600
You can steal the method for any subject, and you do not need to write a single line of code.

00:07:22.600 --> 00:07:30.980
Go to the chatGPT website and type, turn the causes of World War One into eight practice questions that get harder each time.

00:07:31.660 --> 00:07:45.180
Add, do not give answers until I try. Then tell me what I missed and why. Swap in Spanish verbs, sell biology, or whatever is on tomorrow's quiz, and notice how it coaches instead of just dumping an answer key.

00:07:46.180 --> 00:07:51.280
Hollywood just put a fence around something, and a courtroom just learned a hard lesson about chat boxes.

00:07:52.000 --> 00:08:00.700
Bite Dance, the company behind TikTok, signed a written agreement with the Motion Picture Association, the big Hollywood group that looks after movies and shows.

00:08:01.480 --> 00:08:06.700
Bite Dance agreed to pull back how its AI models use Hollywood movies, characters, and stories.

00:08:06.700 --> 00:08:17.600
If you care about the films you love, and about AI that can clone a famous universe in two clicks, this is the entertainment world drawing a line and saying, please do not eat our homework.

00:08:18.340 --> 00:08:24.320
And someone on a jury typed details from a real trial into chatGPT, and the whole trial collapsed.

00:08:24.880 --> 00:08:31.100
Treat that as a flashing neon sign. A chat box is not a private notebook, and it is not a secret diary.

00:08:31.100 --> 00:08:38.740
If something is secret, someone else's business or would get you in trouble on a poster in the hallway, do not paste it into an AI.

00:08:39.380 --> 00:08:47.340
And that's a wrap. If any of today's stories made you go, huh, that's cool. Go play with it. Curiosity is how every expert started.

